  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Quiet Native Terminal",
    "overview": "Torch uses a cool blue-to-lilac sidebar, compact grouped sessions, and a lightly raised terminal pane.\nThe interface keeps session context legible while leaving the working area to shell output. The\nbinding visual direction is the user-supplied [terminal reference](docs/design/terminal-reference.png).\nThe north-star phrase describes that direction; it is not a separate user-approved brand name.\n\nThis is a Rust/WarpUI desktop system. Frontmatter dimensions use portable `px` notation for WarpUI\nlogical pixels, not screenshot pixels. The tokens describe the current implementation in\n`app/src/workspace/view/vertical_tabs/torch.rs`; font and terminal defaults come from\n`app/src/settings/font.rs`, `app/src/settings/theme.rs`, and `app/src/terminal/settings.rs`.\nDefault terminal colors come from `app/src/themes/default_themes.rs`, and UI font loading comes from\n`app/src/appearance.rs`. User-selected terminal themes and fonts remain authoritative at runtime.\n\nThe macOS captures in `.impeccable/review/desktop.png` (2560 \u00d7 1600 screenshot pixels),\n`desktop-narrow.png`, and `desktop-long-title.png` (1730 \u00d7 2046 each, native half-screen windows)\nsupport close visual fidelity, not pixel identity. Their explicitly labelled synthetic transcript is\na visual test fixture, not evidence of a completed CLI task. The dark palette is extracted from\nsource; these captures do not verify dark rendering or other operating systems. Native recording\nbadges and cursor halos are capture artifacts, not Torch components.\n\nThe final macOS live verification passed short reordering within a manual group, a long drag out\nand back, Shift-selection across noncontiguous underlying tabs in displayed group order, and\nreopening the sidebar after restoring its stored closed state. The final drop resolves the dragged\ntab by stable identity before applying its position. These results supplement the source-derived\ninteraction rules below; screenshots alone do not verify interactions.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Compact session navigation with restrained activity markers.",
      "Tonal sidebar depth and one inset terminal surface.",
      "Native UI typography beside configurable monospace output.",
      "Session content and status derived from application state."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The State Evidence Rule",
        "body": "Color and status symbols represent observed application state, never\ninvented activity, sample project progress, or presumed CLI success.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Output Ownership Rule",
        "body": "Terminal programs own their text, ANSI colors, wrapping, and content.\nDo not reconstruct their output as product cards or embed a built-in assistant transcript.",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do preserve the binding terminal reference and the compact native hierarchy.",
      "Do keep user titles, working directories, activity, and unread state tied to real application data.",
      "Do preserve explicit groups and use the displayed group order for range selection.",
      "Do keep terminal themes, font preferences, shell completion, and full-screen programs functional.",
      "Do distinguish source-derived styling, visual fixtures, and live behavior verification."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't add built-in AI, account, billing, telemetry, or cloud controls to this local terminal.",
      "Don't fabricate session activity or treat a quiet check as guaranteed command success.",
      "Don't reproduce the operating system's recording badge or capture cursor halo as product UI.",
      "Don't claim pixel identity, dark-mode visual verification, or untested platform coverage.",
      "Don't replace terminal output with static product-owned conversation content."
    ]
  }
}
